window.SLIDE_TEXT["echo-telemetry"] = {"1":"<ul><li>In Depth Review on Cardiac Testing and Procedures</li><li>Kettering College</li><li>Presented by Jacob Sillaman MPAS, PA-C</li></ul>",
"2":"<ul><li>Patient presents complaining of increased dyspnea and swelling. On physical exam you note 1+ lower extremity edema and a grade 2/6 systolic murmur. What test would you order if you suspect valvular heart disease?</li><li>ECG</li><li>Echocardiogram</li><li>Exercise Treadmill stress test with myoview</li><li>Holter monitor</li></ul>",
"3":"",
"4":"<ul><li>Objectives</li><li>Know the difference between transthoracic and transesophageal echocardiogram</li><li>Understand when to order an echo</li><li>Know what structures are evaluated during an echocardiogram</li><li>Know when to order a TEE vs TTE</li><li>Know which valve is best visualized during a TEE</li><li>Know the contraindications to performing a TEE</li><li>Know the possible complications of at TEE</li><li>Understand the basic concepts of a MUGA scan</li><li>Know when to order telemetry</li><li>Know when to order an implantable monitoring device</li></ul>",
"5":"<ul><li>Transthoracic Echocardiogram (TTE)</li><li>Primary noninvasive imaging modality for quantitative and qualitative evaluation of cardiac anatomy and function</li><li>Structural components of the heart<ul><li>Biventricular function (left ventricular systolic function, diastolic function, right ventricular function)</li><li>Structure abnormalities (left ventricular hypertrophy, wall motion abnormalities, septal aneurysm, PFO, ASD)</li><li>Four heart valves; aortic, mitral, tricuspid, pulmonic</li></ul></li></ul>",
"6":"<ul><li>Echocardiogram</li><li>Imaging windows</li><li>1. Parasternal</li><li>2. Apical</li><li>3. Subcostal (subxyphoid)</li></ul>",
"7":"<ul><li>Parasternal views</li><li>Long axis – useful to estimate the size and contractility of the right and left ventricle (septum and posterior wall), to assess the morphology and function of the mitral and aortic valves. With color Doppler you can look for an aortic or mitral regurgitation and try to determine their mechanism.</li><li>Short axis - There are 3 levels of imaging the heart in short axis:<ul><li>Base of the heart, level of the aortic valve.</li><li>Level of the mitral valve</li><li>Mid-ventricle: papillary muscles and toward the apex</li></ul></li></ul>",
"8":"<ul><li>Apical views</li><li>4 chamber view - In this view you will be able to visualize the 4 main chambers of the heart: left ventricle and atrium, right ventricle and atrium. The Apical 4 chamber view is the best view to calculate the Simpson ejection fraction, to visualize the apex of the left ventricle, and to study the mitral inflow (diastolic function and mitral stenosis)</li><li>5 chamber view - The apical 5 chamber view differs from the apical 4 chamber view by the presence of the aortic valve in the center of the image. This is the view where you will get the best alignement of your Doppler beam with the aortic outflow tract, to look and grade an aortic stenosis and to calculate the cardiac output.</li><li>2 and 3 chamber - The apical 2 chamber view will show you the anterior and inferior walls of the left ventricle.</li></ul>",
"9":"<ul><li>Subcostal Views</li><li>4 chamber - In some ventilated patients, the subcostal view will be the only window accessible. You will be able to assess LV and RV function and valves regurgitations</li><li>Short axis - From a subcostal approach, it is possible to obtain a short axis of the left ventricle, to estimate LV function, and a short axis of the aortic valve with visualization of the tricuspid and pulmonic valves.</li><li>Inferior vena cava - Determination of the IVC diameter and respiratory variations are pivotal for the estimation of a patient's volume status.</li></ul>",
"10":"<ul><li>Echocardiography</li><li>M-mode echocardiography - Provides a “tracing” of the heart structures.</li><li>Useful for measuring pumping chambers, size and thickness of the heart walls.</li></ul>",
"11":"<ul><li>Echocardiography</li><li>Doppler  echocardiography -Measures the flow of blood through the heart's chambers and valves.</li><li>Color Doppler - Color Doppler is an enhanced form of Doppler echocardiography. With color Doppler, different colors are used to designate the direction of blood flow. This simplifies the interpretation of the Doppler technique.</li></ul>",
"12":"<ul><li>Echocardiography</li><li>2-D echocardiography - This technique is used to visualize the actual structures and motion of the heart structures.</li><li>Real-time motion of the heart's structures can be observed.</li></ul>",
"13":"<ul><li>Echocardiogram</li><li>3-D echocardiography. This technique captures three-dimensional views of the heart structures with greater depth than 2-D echo. The live or \"real time\" images allow for a more accurate assessment of heart function by using measurements taken while the heart is beating.</li><li>Useful for CT surgeon prior to surgery.</li></ul>",
"14":"<ul><li>Echocardiography</li><li>Left ventricular ejection fraction</li><li>Left ventricle outflow tract</li><li>Stroke volume</li><li>Diastolic function</li><li>End diastolic volume</li><li>Left ventricular hypertrophy</li><li>Pulmonary artery systolic pressure</li><li>IVC size</li><li>Aortic root size</li><li>Left and right atria size and dimensions</li><li>Aortic valve area</li><li>Aortic valve peak velocity</li><li>Mitral valve stenosis</li><li>Mitral valve regurgitation</li><li>Mitral valve prolapse</li><li>Patent foramen ovale</li><li>Atrial septal defect</li><li>Tricuspid regurgitation</li><li>Pulmonic stenosis</li><li>Longitudinal strain</li></ul>",
"15":"<ul><li>Transesophageal Echocardiography (TEE)</li></ul>",
"16":"<ul><li>TEE Indications</li><li>Suspected acute aortic pathology (ie, dissection, transsection, intramural hematoma)</li><li>Suspected prosthetic valve dysfunction (thrombus, pannus ingrowth, vegetation, or regurgitation)</li><li>Suspected complications of endocarditis (eg, fistula, abscess)</li><li>Evaluation for left atrial/LAA thrombus in a patient with atrial fibrillation/atrial flutter to facilitate clinical decision making regarding anticoagulation, cardioversion, or ablation</li><li>To diagnose infective endocarditis in patients with moderate or high pretest likelihood (ie, patients with prosthetic valves, certain pathogens)</li><li>To evaluate for cardiac source of embolus</li><li>As follow-up to prior TEE when an interval change would result in a change in therapy (eg, resolution of vegetation following antimicrobial therapy)</li><li>To evaluate for cardiac pathology when transthoracic echocardiography is nondiagnostic</li><li>To facilitate clinical decision making regarding anticoagulation, cardioversion, or ablation in patients with atrial fibrillation/flutter To provide guidance for noncoronary percutaneous cardiac interventions (eg, placement of closure devices, valvuloplasty, percutaneous valves)</li></ul>",
"17":"<ul><li>Contraindications (absolute)</li><li>Previous esophagectomy or esophagogastrectomy</li></ul>",
"18":"<ul><li>Contraindications (relative)</li><li>Recent active esophageal tear or hemorrhage</li><li>Esophageal stricture</li><li>Coagulopathies</li><li>Thrombocytopenia</li><li>Zenker’s diverticulum</li><li>Altered mental status</li><li>Severe cervical spine arthritis (reduce range of motion leads to increased risk of complications)</li></ul>",
"19":"<ul><li>Complications</li><li>Esophageal perforation</li><li>Gastrointestinal bleeding – An increased risk of bleeding should be considered among patients with thrombocytopenia (platelet count &lt;50 k),</li><li>Pharyngeal hematoma</li><li>Methemoglobinemia – Methemoglobinemia is a rare but potentially life-threatening complication of topical benzocaine and related agents used for posterior pharyngeal anesthesia</li><li>Minor complications:<ul><li>Transient bronchospasm</li><li>Transient hypoxia</li><li>NSVT</li><li>AF</li><li>Hemoptysis</li><li>Vomiting</li></ul></li></ul>",
"20":"<ul><li>TEE vs TTE</li><li>Transthoracic echo remains the initial test of choice for most patients requiring an echocardiogram. However, a transesophageal echo should be performed as the initial test in certain life-threatening situations or in situations where transthoracic echo is likely to be non-diagnostic</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>TEE</td><td>TTE</td></tr><tr><td>Superior visualization of posterior structures (best view of mitral valve)</td><td>Quick</td></tr><tr><td>Minimally invasive</td><td>Non-invasive</td></tr><tr><td>Requires skilled physician</td><td>Performed by echo tech</td></tr></table>",
"21":"<ul><li>Bubble Study</li><li>Contrast echo can be useful for confirming a diagnosis of atrial septal defect (ASD) or patent foramen ovale (PFO). Agitating saline or synthetic contrast create microbubbles. These are very reflective, and when injected intravenously can be seen as opacification in the echo window. They are normally seen on the right side of the heart before being trapped and absorbed by the pulmonary capillaries, so have no route to the left side of the heart. The contrast created by the bubbles allows a left-to-right shunt to be seen as a jet \"interrupting\" the opacification of the right atrium. However, there is a theoretical risk of systemic air embolism with a right-to-left shunt.</li><li>http://reference.medscape.com/features/slideshow/congenital-heart-disease</li></ul>",
"22":"<ul><li>Multi Gated Acquisition Scan (MUGA)</li><li>Radionuclide ventriculography</li><li>Volumetric measurement</li><li>Red blood pool is labeled using a radioactive tracer and radioactivity is measured over anterior chest using a gamma camera.</li></ul>",
"23":"<ul><li>MUGA scan</li><li>Preferred technique for measurement of the left ventricular ejection fraction with high degree of accuracy</li><li>Very accurate and reproducible</li><li>Body habitus does not limit technique</li><li>Non-invasive</li><li>Radiation exposure – 620 mrem</li><li>Contraindicated in pregnancy or lactating women</li></ul>",
"24":"<ul><li>Case Study 2</li><li>66 y/o female presents complaining of “thumping heart beat” with associated shortness of breath. Palpitation occur daily. Complains of daily fatigue.</li></ul>",
"25":"<ul><li>Cardiac Telemetry</li><li>Continuous monitoring of the heart’s electrical activity</li><li>View of two or three leads of an ECG</li><li>Indicated for:<ul><li>Suspected arrhythmia</li><li>Unexplained syncope</li><li>Evaluation of therapeutic intervention</li></ul></li></ul>",
"26":"<ul><li>Holter Monitor</li><li>24 and 48 hour holter monitors</li><li>Allows physician to obtain a continuous ECG</li><li>Holter monitor is placed in outpatient office</li><li>Patient is given symptom diary.</li><li>Drops holter monitor off at physician office after 24/48 hour period.</li><li>http://www.spiddalmedicalcentre.com/24-hr-holter-monitor/</li></ul>",
"27":"<ul><li>30 Day Event Monitor</li><li>Monitor patient’s rhythm for 2-4 weeks</li><li>Used for high index of suspicion for arrhythmia, detect afib burden, etc…</li><li>Mailed to patient’s house with instructions and later returned to office</li><li>Patient is allowed to remove event monitor to shower</li><li>Uncomfortable/patient compliance</li></ul>",
"28":"<ul><li>Implantable Monitoring Device</li><li>Indicated for unexplained syncope and cryptogenic stroke</li><li>Can monitor rhythm for up to 3 years</li><li>Minimally invasive procedure</li><li>Rhythm downloads each night</li><li>Needs to explanted after three years</li><li>Difficult to get insurance company approval</li></ul>"};
